WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.620
In this video, you raise the overcommit ratios in SUSE Virtualization and create ten small virtual machines at once, so each one reserves less of its host.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:17.530
In Advanced, Settings, overcommit-config sets how much CPU, memory, and storage the scheduler may promise beyond physical capacity.

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.930
Open its action menu,

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:22.850
and select Edit Setting.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:27.240
For this demo, raise CPU from one thousand to four thousand percent,

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.320
memory from one hundred fifty to three hundred percent,

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.790
and storage to four hundred percent. These values are deliberately exaggerated.

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:37.270
Select Save.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.770
Now go to Virtual Machines

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:50.020
and select Create.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.690
Select Multiple Instance.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.590
Choose the namespace,

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.450
a name prefix,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.720
and a count of ten, the most the form creates at once.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.990
Give each one CPU

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:05.530
and one gigabyte of memory.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:07.380
On the Volumes tab,

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:12.450
select the openSUSE Leap ARM64 image

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:19.230
and shrink the root disk to two gigabytes. Volumes are thin-provisioned, so they hold only what the guest writes.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:26.500
Select Create. The form creates the ten virtual machines one after another.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:35.610
All ten virtual machines are running. With three hundred percent memory overcommit, each one requests only a third of its memory from the scheduler.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:46.262
Overcommit lets each host admit more small virtual machines, at the cost of contention under load. Restore the default ratios when you finish testing.
